<article class="env-remark" id="0GZ5">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0GZ5">Remark <span data-tag="0GZ5">23.12.2</span>.</a> In the situation above, if $R$ is Noetherian, we can inductively choose a sequence of integers $1 = n_0 < n_1 < n_2 < \ldots $ such that for $i = 1, 2, 3, \ldots $ we have maps $K_{n_ i} \to A_ i \to R/(f_1^{n_ i}, \ldots , f_ r^{n_ i})$ and $A_ i \to K_{n_{i - 1}}$ as in Lemma <a href="/tag/0GZ4" data-tag="0GZ4">23.12.1</a>. Denote $A_{i + 1} \to A_ i$ the composition $A_{i + 1} \to K_{n_ i} \to A_ i$. Then the diagram </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ K_{n_1} \ar[d] &  K_{n_2} \ar[d] \ar[l] &  K_{n_3} \ar[d] \ar[l] &  \ldots \ar[l] \\  A_1 \ar[d] &  A_2 \ar[l] \ar[d] &  A_3 \ar[l] \ar[d] &  \ldots \ar[l] \\  K_1 &  K_{n_1} \ar[l] &  K_{n_2} \ar[l] &  \ldots \ar[l] }  \]
</div>
  <p> commutes. In this way we see that the inverse systems $(K_ n)$ and $(A_ n)$ are pro-isomorphic in the category of differential graded $R$-algebras with compatible divided powers. </p>
</article>